The upper bounds can be improved for trees.





\begin{rem}\label{dom2c}
Let $G$ be a graph that  has at least two components $G_1$ and $G_2$.
Then $\gamma(\Gc) \leq 2$, because $\{w_1, w_2\}$ is a dominating set of $\Gc$ for
 $w_1 \in G_1, w_2 \in G_2$.    Therefore, $\gamma(\Gc) \leq 2$.
\end{rem}

\begin{prop}\label{kappapd}
Suppose  $G$ is a graph with $\diam (G)=2$ that does not have a dominating vertex.  If $\kappa(G)\ge 2$ then $\pd (G)\leq  \kappa (G)-1$ or $\pd (\Gc)\leq 2$; otherwise $\kappa(G)=1$ and $\pd (\Gc)\leq 2$. 
\end{prop} \vspace{-12pt}

\begin{proof} Let $S$ be a minimum disconnecting set for $G$. Since $\diam (G)=2$, every vertex in $V\setminus S$ is adjacent to at least one  vertex in $S$. 
\\ \underline{Case 1:} There exists  a vertex $u\in V\setminus S$ that is adjacent to exactly one vertex in $S$, say $v$ (Case 1 is the only possible case when $\kappa(G)=1$). Let $G_1$ denote the connected component of $G-S$ containing $u$.  
  In $\Gc$, $u$ dominates $S\setminus \{v\}$ and all vertices in components of $G-S$ other than $G_1$. Let $x$ be any vertex in a component of $G-S$ that is not equal to $G_1$.  Then $x$ dominates the vertices $G_1$. Therefore,  $\{u,x\}$ dominates all vertices in $V$ except possibly $v$.  Since $G$ does not have a dominating vertex, every vertex has a neighbor  in $\Gc$, and any neighbor of $v$ can force $v$, so $\{u,x\}$ is a power dominating set for $\Gc$. Thus, $\pd (\Gc)\leq  2$.
\\ \underline{Case 2:} Every vertex in $V\setminus S$ is adjacent to  at least two vertices in $S$.  Then $S\setminus \{v\}$ is a power dominating set for any vertex $v\in S$: Clearly $S\setminus \{v\}$ dominates $V\setminus \{v\}$.  %Since $G$ does not have a dominating vertex, every vertex has a neighbor  in $\Gc$, and such a 
Any neighbor if $v$ in $\Gc$ can force $v$. Thus, $\pd (G)\leq  \kappa (G)-1$.
\end{proof}


codex
The paper’s native constructions already expose an important distinction: its extremal families (paired leaves and necklace graphs) come with witnesses, but those witnesses are also recoverable from the visible gadget decomposition. I’m checking the algorithmic literature now; if that decomposition makes the proposed distribution efficiently recognizable, it cannot be claimed as Track A and would need a genuine Track B operation gap.
