codex
Built a self-contained Track B generator from the paper’s necklace graphs and verified all local gates.

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1610.03115/gen_1610_03115.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1610.03115/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1610.03115/README.md)
- [Oracle transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1610.03115/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1610.03115/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/1610.03115/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

Key results:

- All G1–G9 local gates pass.
- Structured guessing: 0/200,000; exact density \(1.606\times10^{-92}\).
- Four attacks: each 0/8.
- Honest Track B reference algorithm: 8/8, 15,936 operations, about 0.001 seconds.
- Canonical-key checks: 80/80 invariant, 20/20 unrelated instances distinct.
- Shipping answer: 1,225 characters, 307 estimated tokens, 249 atoms.
- Construction follows Remark 2.16, Theorem 2.17, Lemma 2.18, and Theorem 3.13 of the [paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.03115).

The sole blocker is external: every OpenRouter attempt returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded.” The three transcripts are genuine harness-produced error records, but they are not valid hardness evidence. Consequently, the directory is not submission-ready until the OpenRouter key is replenished and the bare/hinted/placebo runs are repeated.
