/tmp/tmp.e6ZuziTSpx/NGpowerdomination_nolinenumbers.tex:665:\section{Nordhaus-Gaddum product bounds for power domination}\label{sNGprod}
/tmp/tmp.e6ZuziTSpx/NGpowerdomination_nolinenumbers.tex:667:As with the sum, the tight product lower bound for the power domination number for all graphs $G$ remains unchanged even with the additional requirement that both $G$ and $\Gc$ be  connected (using the path).  In Section \ref{sNGsum}, we achieved a tight sum upper bound for such graphs. However, since this was achieved with $\pd(\Gc)=1$ for both $G$ and $\Gc$ connected, and with $\pd(\Gc)=2$ when each component of both $G$ and $\Gc$ has order at least 3, there are few immediate implications for products (see  Section \ref{sdiscuss} for further discussion of connections between sum and product bounds).  
/tmp/tmp.e6ZuziTSpx/NGpowerdomination_nolinenumbers.tex:682:The next result follows from  Corollary \ref{pdle2}  and Theorem \ref{n/3boundcor}.
/tmp/tmp.e6ZuziTSpx/NGpowerdomination_nolinenumbers.tex:708:The next two results are  product analogs of Theorems \ref{smallcomp} and  \ref{n4sum}.  The proofs, which are analogous, are omitted.
/tmp/tmp.e6ZuziTSpx/NGpowerdomination_nolinenumbers.tex:727:The next result follows immediately from Theorem  \ref{3regNGsum}.
/tmp/tmp.e6ZuziTSpx/NGpowerdomination_nolinenumbers.tex:739:\bpf Note first that since $G$ is connected, $\pd(G) \le \lf\frac{n}{3}\rf$ by Theorem \ref{n/3boundcor}.   If a tree is not   a star, then its complement is also connected, and by Proposition \ref{GBarBd1}, $\pd(\Gc) = 1$.    For a star graph $K_{1,n-1}$, we have $\pd(K_{1,n-1})\cdot\pd(\overline{K_{1,n-1}})=2$, which is less than or equal to $\frac{n}{3}$ when $n \ge 6$. 
/tmp/tmp.e6ZuziTSpx/NGpowerdomination_nolinenumbers.tex:751:Table \ref{sumtab} summarizes what is known about Nordhaus-Gaddum sum bounds for power domination number, domination number, and zero forcing number.  
/tmp/tmp.e6ZuziTSpx/NGpowerdomination_nolinenumbers.tex:783:Both the sum and product upper and lower bounds for the domination number were determined by Jaeger and Payan in 1972 (see Theorem \ref{NGdom}), and analogous bounds for power domination are immediate corollaries.  Since then, there have been numerous improvements to the sum upper bound for domination number under various conditions on $G$ and $\Gc$.  Examples of such conditions include requiring every component of both   $G$ and $\Gc$ to have order at least 2 or requiring both to be connected %(see Theorem \ref {NGdomJA95}), 
/tmp/tmp.e6ZuziTSpx/NGpowerdomination_nolinenumbers.tex:784:or requiring both to have minimum degree at least  7. % (see Theorem  \ref{n3dom}).  
